WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.100 --> 00:00:04.480
KIP SCHAUER: All right, welcome.

00:00:04.480 --> 00:00:06.370
My name's Kip Schauer.

00:00:06.370 --> 00:00:11.290
I'm from Denver, Colorado
over in the United States.

00:00:11.290 --> 00:00:14.610
How many people have
been to Colorado before?

00:00:14.610 --> 00:00:20.680
One, all right-- two,
all right-- three.

00:00:20.680 --> 00:00:21.220
Well, great.

00:00:21.220 --> 00:00:23.890
You're all welcome to
come to Colorado some time

00:00:23.890 --> 00:00:28.360
and come enjoy the skiing
or the nice weather we have.

00:00:28.360 --> 00:00:30.100
I've really enjoyed
my trip to Milan.

00:00:30.100 --> 00:00:32.409
This was the first time
I've ever been here.

00:00:32.409 --> 00:00:35.510
I've been to Rome, and
Florence in Venice before.

00:00:35.510 --> 00:00:41.020
So thanks so much for
having me over here.

00:00:41.020 --> 00:00:43.900
So we'll spend the
next 35 minutes--

00:00:43.900 --> 00:00:46.180
and I'm going to save a
little time for questions

00:00:46.180 --> 00:00:49.810
at the end-- talking about
Google's media story,

00:00:49.810 --> 00:00:52.420
so media and entertainment.

00:00:52.420 --> 00:00:55.900
I was hired three
months ago by Google.

00:00:55.900 --> 00:00:59.680
I've spent the last
10 years at Aspera.

00:00:59.680 --> 00:01:01.390
How many people use
Aspera if you're

00:01:01.390 --> 00:01:05.480
in the media and entertainment
world moving large files?

00:01:05.480 --> 00:01:09.940
OK, most people would.

00:01:09.940 --> 00:01:12.160
So then IBM acquired Aspera.

00:01:12.160 --> 00:01:15.250
So for the last three years,
I was over it at IBM also.

00:01:18.380 --> 00:01:21.260
So today we're, going
to talk about media,

00:01:21.260 --> 00:01:24.380
Google's strategy in the
media, and then we're

00:01:24.380 --> 00:01:30.530
going to dig into the online
platform called Anvato, as well

00:01:30.530 --> 00:01:32.430
as the machine learning.

00:01:32.430 --> 00:01:35.930
You might have already
gone to a few sessions

00:01:35.930 --> 00:01:37.460
on machine learning.

00:01:37.460 --> 00:01:39.680
And I'll talk to
you about how we're

00:01:39.680 --> 00:01:44.090
going to be applying it in the
media and entertainment space.

00:01:44.090 --> 00:01:46.790
So a little
background, everybody

00:01:46.790 --> 00:01:50.210
knows that media
consumption is changing.

00:01:50.210 --> 00:01:55.190
Obviously, on my phone here,
I can launch an application,

00:01:55.190 --> 00:01:57.802
watch video content right here.

00:01:57.802 --> 00:01:59.510
How many people were
watching video right

00:01:59.510 --> 00:02:01.920
before they started this?

00:02:01.920 --> 00:02:03.840
Just about everybody, right?

00:02:03.840 --> 00:02:09.690
I read a statistic that, every
single day, 57% of people

00:02:09.690 --> 00:02:12.650
watch video on their phone.

00:02:12.650 --> 00:02:15.600
And my question
to that was, what

00:02:15.600 --> 00:02:18.540
are the other 43% of the
people doing with their phone

00:02:18.540 --> 00:02:21.390
if you're not watching video?

00:02:21.390 --> 00:02:24.510
I mean, everybody
watches video today.

00:02:24.510 --> 00:02:27.130
And some of the videos even
just start to play right away.

00:02:27.130 --> 00:02:30.780
So if you're watching YouTube,
or Facebook, or ESPN or any

00:02:30.780 --> 00:02:34.740
of your sporting channels, the
videos will just start to play.

00:02:34.740 --> 00:02:41.030
So the idea is, how do you make
sure you get the right video

00:02:41.030 --> 00:02:45.150
content to each of us?

00:02:45.150 --> 00:02:49.110
And you have to be
able to get it any time

00:02:49.110 --> 00:02:51.390
to any different device.

00:02:51.390 --> 00:02:53.460
I've got two teenage boys.

00:02:53.460 --> 00:02:57.314
And to get them to sit
down and watch a TV show,

00:02:57.314 --> 00:02:58.980
I don't remember the
last time they did.

00:02:58.980 --> 00:03:03.810
If it was, I'm sure it was
just a movie that was recorded.

00:03:03.810 --> 00:03:09.300
One of my sons has
built this big computer

00:03:09.300 --> 00:03:11.371
with two different monitors.

00:03:11.371 --> 00:03:13.620
And I don't know if he has
a split brain or something.

00:03:13.620 --> 00:03:16.890
But one, he keeps YouTube
and video channels up,

00:03:16.890 --> 00:03:20.790
so he can be watching TV while
he's usually playing a game,

00:03:20.790 --> 00:03:23.970
or doing his homework,
or something like that.

00:03:23.970 --> 00:03:26.430
So the way we're
consuming content

00:03:26.430 --> 00:03:29.010
has changed dramatically.

00:03:29.010 --> 00:03:32.730
And then the other thing for
you take somebody like my son

00:03:32.730 --> 00:03:37.920
is, how does he get to the
content that he wants to watch?

00:03:37.920 --> 00:03:40.712
So he's into paintball.

00:03:40.712 --> 00:03:42.420
I don't know if they
have paintball here.

00:03:42.420 --> 00:03:46.410
But you run around and shoot
each other with paintball guns.

00:03:46.410 --> 00:03:50.290
And there's actually a
league in North America.

00:03:50.290 --> 00:03:53.610
So there's a professional
group of paintballers

00:03:53.610 --> 00:03:55.380
in North America.

00:03:55.380 --> 00:03:58.080
And they have a
championship game.

00:03:58.080 --> 00:04:03.060
And you can actually pay a
company that livestreams it

00:04:03.060 --> 00:04:06.960
like $5.00 a month to be
able to watch those games.

00:04:06.960 --> 00:04:08.790
I mean, you start
to think about that,

00:04:08.790 --> 00:04:12.420
and what it opens up for
the possibility of everybody

00:04:12.420 --> 00:04:17.310
is being able to watch video,
live video, livestreaming

00:04:17.310 --> 00:04:25.530
video for whatever hobby, or
interest, or special concept

00:04:25.530 --> 00:04:30.750
that we have out there, and be
able to consume it on my phone

00:04:30.750 --> 00:04:33.930
while I'm at a conference,
be able to watch it at home

00:04:33.930 --> 00:04:37.980
potentially on a TV,
or on your computer

00:04:37.980 --> 00:04:40.590
have two different
monitors going.

00:04:40.590 --> 00:04:42.630
And you can consume the content.

00:04:42.630 --> 00:04:45.960
So everybody that's in the
media and entertainment world,

00:04:45.960 --> 00:04:51.300
you know that the trick is
getting it to every device now.

00:04:51.300 --> 00:04:54.390
So then the next
step is, how do I

00:04:54.390 --> 00:04:58.290
use data to drive what
content's being pushed?

00:04:58.290 --> 00:05:02.940
And how do I personalize it,
so that we each are consuming

00:05:02.940 --> 00:05:05.700
the video content that we want?

00:05:05.700 --> 00:05:08.340
"House of Cards," I'm
not sure how popular

00:05:08.340 --> 00:05:09.520
that show was over here.

00:05:09.520 --> 00:05:11.370
It was a Netflix show.

00:05:11.370 --> 00:05:15.990
But they used data
to figure out who

00:05:15.990 --> 00:05:19.950
was going to watch the series
before they launched it

00:05:19.950 --> 00:05:22.380
by running different
types of trailers,

00:05:22.380 --> 00:05:26.880
and testing them, getting all
that data back, analyzing it.

00:05:26.880 --> 00:05:29.380
And then it helped them
launch "House of Cards."

00:05:29.380 --> 00:05:32.690
And it became a very,
very popular show.

00:05:32.690 --> 00:05:35.530
A couple of the other
things that are interesting,

00:05:35.530 --> 00:05:37.920
the amount of content
now that's available

00:05:37.920 --> 00:05:42.450
is, how do I get my content seen
if I'm on the other side trying

00:05:42.450 --> 00:05:44.430
to produce the content?

00:05:44.430 --> 00:05:47.310
There's over 400 hours
of video uploaded

00:05:47.310 --> 00:05:51.360
to YouTube every minute, so
just massive, massive amounts

00:05:51.360 --> 00:05:54.020
of content being uploaded.

00:05:54.020 --> 00:05:57.830
And then with machine
learning coming out,

00:05:57.830 --> 00:06:01.790
people are thinking-- well,
machine learning a little bit,

00:06:01.790 --> 00:06:04.880
but also reality TV,
and the YouTube-type

00:06:04.880 --> 00:06:08.330
content or Facebook-type
content that's all reality.

00:06:08.330 --> 00:06:11.240
I know, a few years
ago, everybody

00:06:11.240 --> 00:06:14.900
was thinking that TV
shows that are scripted

00:06:14.900 --> 00:06:18.730
that have actors and actresses
were kind of going to go away.

00:06:18.730 --> 00:06:23.090
And the world was just going
to evolve into reality TV.

00:06:23.090 --> 00:06:26.510
What's happened is actually
the complete opposite,

00:06:26.510 --> 00:06:32.210
where today there's over
455 scripted TV shows.

00:06:32.210 --> 00:06:35.930
That's over double what we
had just five years ago.

00:06:35.930 --> 00:06:39.470
I was down in Los
Angeles two weeks ago.

00:06:39.470 --> 00:06:41.940
And in Los Angeles, that's
where a lot of the--

00:06:41.940 --> 00:06:45.800
they call them sound stages,
where they actually shoot

00:06:45.800 --> 00:06:49.620
the TV shows and film them.

00:06:49.620 --> 00:06:53.060
And right now, every
single sound stage

00:06:53.060 --> 00:06:58.340
is full, both on the
Universal lot, Disney, Fox.

00:06:58.340 --> 00:07:00.800
They're renting them out,
because there's so much

00:07:00.800 --> 00:07:03.200
new content being produced.

00:07:03.200 --> 00:07:05.360
And the reason is, is
because you can get that

00:07:05.360 --> 00:07:08.390
to every one of your
consumers without just having

00:07:08.390 --> 00:07:10.130
to go through one
of the big channels

00:07:10.130 --> 00:07:12.050
to distribute your content.

00:07:12.050 --> 00:07:12.950
So it's exciting.

00:07:12.950 --> 00:07:15.230
In my eyes, this is exciting.

00:07:15.230 --> 00:07:18.800
Because it means there's
new content, better content,

00:07:18.800 --> 00:07:20.810
more targeted content.

00:07:20.810 --> 00:07:23.960
And it allows us to
personalize the content that's

00:07:23.960 --> 00:07:28.492
being created and get
that to the consumer.

00:07:28.492 --> 00:07:30.200
I'm not going to spend
much time on this,

00:07:30.200 --> 00:07:33.020
because I'm sure
you've already attended

00:07:33.020 --> 00:07:34.730
some of the other seminars.

00:07:34.730 --> 00:07:38.630
But the investment in
Google's Cloud is amazing.

00:07:38.630 --> 00:07:42.740
For video customers, content
distribution networks

00:07:42.740 --> 00:07:44.060
are critical.

00:07:44.060 --> 00:07:48.080
Being able to stream your
video and have a quick user

00:07:48.080 --> 00:07:52.070
experience are absolutely
imperative to getting

00:07:52.070 --> 00:07:53.660
good-quality video.

00:07:53.660 --> 00:07:58.070
So today, you can use Akamai.

00:07:58.070 --> 00:08:01.130
There's Limelight,
Level3, Fastly.

00:08:01.130 --> 00:08:03.110
There's quite a few
content distribution

00:08:03.110 --> 00:08:04.670
networks available.

00:08:04.670 --> 00:08:09.300
And Google, last year, opened
up the CDN that YouTube runs on.

00:08:09.300 --> 00:08:11.570
So the performance that
you get on YouTube,

00:08:11.570 --> 00:08:14.690
the scalability of
YouTube, is now available.

00:08:14.690 --> 00:08:18.830
So for a video
content publisher,

00:08:18.830 --> 00:08:21.140
you're going to have
another option of a content

00:08:21.140 --> 00:08:22.730
distribution network.

00:08:22.730 --> 00:08:27.350
It's mainly been internally
consumed by the YouTube team.

00:08:27.350 --> 00:08:30.860
So it might not have all
the bells and whistles

00:08:30.860 --> 00:08:32.600
right now that you
would expect if you're

00:08:32.600 --> 00:08:36.080
used to a commercially-facing
content distribution network.

00:08:36.080 --> 00:08:38.570
But it's definitely
ramping up quickly.

00:08:38.570 --> 00:08:42.590
So if you're using a content
distribution network today,

00:08:42.590 --> 00:08:44.570
you're looking at
moving over to Google,

00:08:44.570 --> 00:08:48.380
you'll want to look at Google
as a possible CDN provider

00:08:48.380 --> 00:08:49.460
for your video content.

00:08:53.150 --> 00:08:55.640
Obviously, I think everybody
heard in the keynote,

00:08:55.640 --> 00:09:01.180
we have the applications that
run over a billion users.

00:09:01.180 --> 00:09:04.520
So that brings us back to
media and entertainment.

00:09:04.520 --> 00:09:07.180
Where is our focus?

00:09:07.180 --> 00:09:10.600
So in kind of step
1, we have customers

00:09:10.600 --> 00:09:14.020
that just want to lift and
shift their applications

00:09:14.020 --> 00:09:15.610
over to the Cloud.

00:09:15.610 --> 00:09:18.880
Over there, you might be using
just the compute environment.

00:09:18.880 --> 00:09:23.830
You might be using the storage
environment, as well as

00:09:23.830 --> 00:09:25.970
the content
distribution network.

00:09:25.970 --> 00:09:28.210
So this morning, I
met with a customer.

00:09:28.210 --> 00:09:30.310
They thought that
they wanted to run

00:09:30.310 --> 00:09:32.640
on top of our video
streaming platform.

00:09:32.640 --> 00:09:34.390
But it really turned
out, they had already

00:09:34.390 --> 00:09:36.142
built everything they needed.

00:09:36.142 --> 00:09:38.350
And they were just going to
need to lift and shift it

00:09:38.350 --> 00:09:42.760
up over onto the
infrastructure platform.

00:09:42.760 --> 00:09:47.680
So when you're working through
media and entertainment

00:09:47.680 --> 00:09:54.280
challenges, you'll
find that, oftentimes,

00:09:54.280 --> 00:09:58.510
what you've already built
might move over onto the Cloud

00:09:58.510 --> 00:10:01.630
pretty easily and then just
be able to take advantage

00:10:01.630 --> 00:10:04.000
of the different CDN options.

00:10:04.000 --> 00:10:07.780
So we can definitely help
you move your applications

00:10:07.780 --> 00:10:09.340
over to the Cloud.

00:10:09.340 --> 00:10:12.420
Then in the middle,
basic over-the-top

00:10:12.420 --> 00:10:15.490
app-- so Google acquired
a company called Anvato.

00:10:15.490 --> 00:10:19.870
And I'll talk at a deeper
level on Anvato back in July.

00:10:19.870 --> 00:10:22.780
That can provide you an
end-to-end over-the-top

00:10:22.780 --> 00:10:23.650
solution.

00:10:23.650 --> 00:10:26.110
So if you're looking at
launching your own application

00:10:26.110 --> 00:10:30.200
and you need video content
in there, we'll go over that.

00:10:30.200 --> 00:10:33.940
Re-engineering the workflow--
so if you have a workflow where

00:10:33.940 --> 00:10:36.370
you might be able to scale it
up quicker if you're running

00:10:36.370 --> 00:10:39.904
on the Cloud, we have people
that can help you figure out,

00:10:39.904 --> 00:10:41.320
how do I move it
over to the Cloud

00:10:41.320 --> 00:10:43.880
and then be able to scale it up?

00:10:43.880 --> 00:10:47.060
And then the third
area that's obviously

00:10:47.060 --> 00:10:50.530
receiving a lot of attention
is the machine learning

00:10:50.530 --> 00:10:53.900
and, how are we going to apply
it into our video products?

00:10:53.900 --> 00:10:57.020
Or how can you take that
Video Intelligence API

00:10:57.020 --> 00:11:00.230
and apply it to
your own content?

00:11:00.230 --> 00:11:02.120
So these are kind
of the three areas

00:11:02.120 --> 00:11:05.570
that we're really focusing
on from a Google perspective

00:11:05.570 --> 00:11:09.960
on the media and
entertainment market.

00:11:09.960 --> 00:11:12.960
So Google's commitment to
the media and entertainment

00:11:12.960 --> 00:11:13.590
market--

00:11:13.590 --> 00:11:16.230
I had been in the media
and entertainment market

00:11:16.230 --> 00:11:18.010
for the last 10 years.

00:11:18.010 --> 00:11:20.020
Obviously, you know
that Google's YouTube.

00:11:20.020 --> 00:11:21.270
And you kind of think of that.

00:11:21.270 --> 00:11:23.850
But they've acquired
a product called

00:11:23.850 --> 00:11:25.590
ZYNC in the rendering engine.

00:11:25.590 --> 00:11:28.980
And they also support many
of the largest companies

00:11:28.980 --> 00:11:31.750
doing the big, animated movies.

00:11:31.750 --> 00:11:36.517
Anvato is a over-the-top
broadcasting solution.

00:11:36.517 --> 00:11:38.100
And I'm going quick
on this, because I

00:11:38.100 --> 00:11:40.020
have additional slides on them.

00:11:40.020 --> 00:11:42.960
Intelligence, we're just
starting the intelligence

00:11:42.960 --> 00:11:43.470
phase.

00:11:43.470 --> 00:11:45.580
That's definitely,
in my opinion,

00:11:45.580 --> 00:11:46.890
we're at the ground level.

00:11:46.890 --> 00:11:51.630
And customers like you are going
to help define the best ways

00:11:51.630 --> 00:11:54.180
to use this technology.

00:11:54.180 --> 00:11:57.390
Monetization, so if you're
playing back to your videos

00:11:57.390 --> 00:11:59.310
and you can add
the ads in there,

00:11:59.310 --> 00:12:01.930
obviously, that's how you're
going to start to make money.

00:12:01.930 --> 00:12:05.460
And then Google acquired
a company called Widevine.

00:12:05.460 --> 00:12:08.640
Widevine is one of
the DRM solutions.

00:12:08.640 --> 00:12:12.360
So there's about three different
Digital Right Management

00:12:12.360 --> 00:12:13.320
solutions out there.

00:12:13.320 --> 00:12:16.800
Google owns Widevine,
YouTube, Chrome, obviously.

00:12:16.800 --> 00:12:20.160
So this is kind of
our 360-degree view

00:12:20.160 --> 00:12:23.870
of media and entertainment.

00:12:23.870 --> 00:12:26.360
So really quick,
for anybody that's

00:12:26.360 --> 00:12:31.860
into 3D rendering out there,
we have an application.

00:12:31.860 --> 00:12:37.860
So if you're using Maya or
Cinema 4D, Houdini, or Nuke,

00:12:37.860 --> 00:12:40.080
those are applications
that look like this that

00:12:40.080 --> 00:12:43.140
allow you to create 3D models.

00:12:43.140 --> 00:12:45.210
We have created a plug-in.

00:12:45.210 --> 00:12:48.030
And you can't really read
this through the projector.

00:12:48.030 --> 00:12:51.840
But it's the coolest,
quick way to use the Cloud.

00:12:51.840 --> 00:12:55.161
So when you've
finished your model

00:12:55.161 --> 00:12:56.910
and you've done all
your coloring, and all

00:12:56.910 --> 00:12:59.010
your frames, and
all your rendering,

00:12:59.010 --> 00:13:01.950
there's a pull-down
menu right here

00:13:01.950 --> 00:13:06.840
that tells you your options
for cloud rendering.

00:13:06.840 --> 00:13:10.960
And it gives you, how
many cores are there?

00:13:10.960 --> 00:13:12.810
And then you literally
just type in a number

00:13:12.810 --> 00:13:14.370
for the number of
computers you want

00:13:14.370 --> 00:13:16.530
to have spun up in the Cloud.

00:13:16.530 --> 00:13:19.140
And it'll tell you
your cost right there.

00:13:19.140 --> 00:13:21.870
We took care of all
the rendering costs

00:13:21.870 --> 00:13:24.540
on the back end, so you don't
have to have special rendering

00:13:24.540 --> 00:13:25.560
engines.

00:13:25.560 --> 00:13:29.730
And when you hit Go, it will
package all your content up,

00:13:29.730 --> 00:13:32.530
send it up to the Cloud,
do your full render,

00:13:32.530 --> 00:13:35.750
and then bring it back down.

00:13:35.750 --> 00:13:40.460
So "The Jungle Book" was
done by a company called MPC.

00:13:40.460 --> 00:13:44.139
And they used the Google Cloud.

00:13:44.139 --> 00:13:45.680
They did not using
ZYNC, because they

00:13:45.680 --> 00:13:48.790
have their own big, custom
workflow for rendering.

00:13:48.790 --> 00:13:52.940
But the amount of compute to
do some of these very, very

00:13:52.940 --> 00:13:57.990
high-end rendering projects
is kind of mind boggling.

00:13:57.990 --> 00:14:00.380
But 41 years of
compute time they

00:14:00.380 --> 00:14:05.900
were able to do in
just over two months.

00:14:05.900 --> 00:14:09.350
So what does this
allow the artists

00:14:09.350 --> 00:14:13.790
in the animation team
that's working on it?

00:14:13.790 --> 00:14:15.500
It gives them more time.

00:14:15.500 --> 00:14:18.980
It allows them to
spend more time

00:14:18.980 --> 00:14:24.080
working on the fine details
and just continually iterating

00:14:24.080 --> 00:14:29.480
on the product, so that they can
deliver a better product to us.

00:14:29.480 --> 00:14:32.060
So that's the power of the
Cloud around rendering.

00:14:32.060 --> 00:14:35.720
So anybody that's in the
rendering portion of media

00:14:35.720 --> 00:14:40.430
and entertainment, definitely
find your local Google

00:14:40.430 --> 00:14:41.690
salesperson.

00:14:41.690 --> 00:14:44.990
We have worked with
all the major studios.

00:14:44.990 --> 00:14:47.820
We have what's called
MPAA certification.

00:14:47.820 --> 00:14:52.730
So the major studios have
approved the Google Cloud--

00:14:52.730 --> 00:14:54.980
so some really amazing
stuff we're doing

00:14:54.980 --> 00:14:58.460
in the rendering area of it.

00:14:58.460 --> 00:15:01.870
So now I'll switch
gears into Anvato.

00:15:01.870 --> 00:15:06.020
Anvato is the company Google
acquired back in July.

00:15:06.020 --> 00:15:10.130
And it's designed to be sort
of an end-to-end over-the-top

00:15:10.130 --> 00:15:13.880
workflow to help you
if you're putting video

00:15:13.880 --> 00:15:15.260
on your application.

00:15:15.260 --> 00:15:19.010
You know, like at the
beginning of the show,

00:15:19.010 --> 00:15:22.160
if you're pulling up
your sports application

00:15:22.160 --> 00:15:24.260
and you need to
stream your video,

00:15:24.260 --> 00:15:30.050
or if you're developing to
be able to stream to, say,

00:15:30.050 --> 00:15:33.680
over Chromecast or
an Apple TV and you

00:15:33.680 --> 00:15:35.810
need to be able to play
your video content back

00:15:35.810 --> 00:15:40.400
on any of those formats, Anvato
will be able to help you.

00:15:40.400 --> 00:15:44.900
Some of the customers, NBC
is the largest broadcaster

00:15:44.900 --> 00:15:46.180
in North America.

00:15:46.180 --> 00:15:48.530
And that's one of
our big customers.

00:15:48.530 --> 00:15:53.110
Univision is Latin America, one
of their largest broadcasters.

00:15:53.110 --> 00:15:56.690
Anvato was North America only.

00:15:56.690 --> 00:15:59.360
So we're starting to work
with European customers

00:15:59.360 --> 00:16:01.490
now that we have the
support of Google

00:16:01.490 --> 00:16:03.740
and have been able
to expand the team.

00:16:03.740 --> 00:16:06.600
So we can definitely
support you.

00:16:06.600 --> 00:16:10.490
We can set up web meetings
if you need to on Anvato.

00:16:10.490 --> 00:16:12.230
So what does Anvato do?

00:16:12.230 --> 00:16:16.520
Again, sort of the complete
end-to-end workflow.

00:16:16.520 --> 00:16:20.760
And I'll dive into it
each little section of it.

00:16:20.760 --> 00:16:22.350
And we'll go through each one.

00:16:22.350 --> 00:16:26.570
So livestreaming,
so you always have

00:16:26.570 --> 00:16:28.340
to have some sort
of hardware to be

00:16:28.340 --> 00:16:30.290
able to capture
the video signal.

00:16:30.290 --> 00:16:33.950
But it's a software-based
encoding engine.

00:16:33.950 --> 00:16:37.550
And you can deploy it either on
premise where the cameras are

00:16:37.550 --> 00:16:39.260
and then move the
data to the Cloud,

00:16:39.260 --> 00:16:42.180
or you can just deploy
it in the Cloud.

00:16:42.180 --> 00:16:45.120
And you can capture
either what's

00:16:45.120 --> 00:16:48.960
called baseband video coming
directly out of the camera.

00:16:48.960 --> 00:16:52.290
Or you can bring in a
previously-encoded signal

00:16:52.290 --> 00:16:55.090
for a live transcode.

00:16:55.090 --> 00:16:57.160
So what does it look like?

00:16:57.160 --> 00:17:00.040
Over there is where you're
capturing your content.

00:17:00.040 --> 00:17:02.590
You're bringing it in
through the Anvato.

00:17:02.590 --> 00:17:05.230
And it's just a
commodity hardware device

00:17:05.230 --> 00:17:07.030
running our software.

00:17:07.030 --> 00:17:10.550
And then you can go straight to
a content distribution network,

00:17:10.550 --> 00:17:12.520
so the CDN.

00:17:12.520 --> 00:17:15.550
And then you can stream
out either in HLS format.

00:17:15.550 --> 00:17:18.740
And we're now releasing DASH,
if you're familiar with DASH.

00:17:18.740 --> 00:17:21.369
It's another option
for streaming video.

00:17:21.369 --> 00:17:22.960
And then the ads
all tie into it.

00:17:22.960 --> 00:17:25.910
But I'll talk about
that in a second.

00:17:25.910 --> 00:17:28.920
If you have a third-party
encoder already,

00:17:28.920 --> 00:17:32.200
meaning you're encoding
your video already

00:17:32.200 --> 00:17:34.540
and you want to send
it to the Cloud,

00:17:34.540 --> 00:17:38.470
so that we can trans code it
into all the different flavors

00:17:38.470 --> 00:17:40.990
and then deliver it
out over your CDN,

00:17:40.990 --> 00:17:42.400
we can run in the Cloud also.

00:17:42.400 --> 00:17:45.520
So you really have two
options for the way

00:17:45.520 --> 00:17:49.030
you run the Anvato software.

00:17:49.030 --> 00:17:50.440
So when that live signal--

00:17:50.440 --> 00:17:52.690
and when I'm
talking live, I mean

00:17:52.690 --> 00:17:56.290
we're watching a newscast
or a sporting event.

00:17:56.290 --> 00:18:02.050
Anvato actually broadcast
the Super Bowl back in 2014,

00:18:02.050 --> 00:18:05.830
so millions and millions of
people watching the same video.

00:18:05.830 --> 00:18:07.390
That's the power of Anvato.

00:18:07.390 --> 00:18:09.870
But then live to
[INAUDIBLE] means--

00:18:09.870 --> 00:18:13.120
so that live signal is coming
in, I'm going to capture it.

00:18:13.120 --> 00:18:17.290
And by capturing it, I can
then use that to post it out

00:18:17.290 --> 00:18:18.910
onto social networks.

00:18:18.910 --> 00:18:21.670
I can push it to
other online sites.

00:18:21.670 --> 00:18:26.840
So being able to capture
it as it's being broadcast

00:18:26.840 --> 00:18:28.100
will save you a step.

00:18:28.100 --> 00:18:30.460
You don't have to wait
until the broadcast is over

00:18:30.460 --> 00:18:32.290
and then have somebody
transfer it to you.

00:18:32.290 --> 00:18:35.590
You can capture it right away.

00:18:35.590 --> 00:18:38.440
So then people say, well,
as it's going along,

00:18:38.440 --> 00:18:41.530
I might need to cut
out advertisements.

00:18:41.530 --> 00:18:44.230
Because sometimes
you can't advertise

00:18:44.230 --> 00:18:46.990
what's going out over
air on your phone

00:18:46.990 --> 00:18:49.340
or on your mobile devices.

00:18:49.340 --> 00:18:51.370
So you might need to
change the advertisement.

00:18:51.370 --> 00:18:54.260
So there's a cloud editing
tool that makes it really,

00:18:54.260 --> 00:18:56.440
really easy to cut ads out.

00:18:56.440 --> 00:18:59.860
And then you can
also quickly grab

00:18:59.860 --> 00:19:02.110
little snippets of highlights.

00:19:02.110 --> 00:19:03.610
And you could use
that to post it

00:19:03.610 --> 00:19:07.600
onto YouTube, Facebook,
Twitter and start

00:19:07.600 --> 00:19:11.910
to send little bits of your
video out to your social media

00:19:11.910 --> 00:19:13.554
quickly.

00:19:13.554 --> 00:19:14.720
So this is the architecture.

00:19:14.720 --> 00:19:16.930
It looks exactly
the same as before.

00:19:16.930 --> 00:19:18.830
It's just now you're
doing your capture

00:19:18.830 --> 00:19:23.210
and your editing in the Cloud
as you receive the content.

00:19:23.210 --> 00:19:25.430
And then you can
also send content out

00:19:25.430 --> 00:19:27.890
to the premium
syndicators, so over here

00:19:27.890 --> 00:19:30.830
like Sky and all
the big broadcasters

00:19:30.830 --> 00:19:33.080
here that need to
receive your content.

00:19:33.080 --> 00:19:38.090
We have over 141
profiles set up worldwide

00:19:38.090 --> 00:19:40.460
to receive the
content, because even

00:19:40.460 --> 00:19:44.000
North-American broadcasters
send the content worldwide.

00:19:44.000 --> 00:19:45.440
So those are already set up.

00:19:45.440 --> 00:19:50.700
They're automated and makes it
easy getting the content out.

00:19:50.700 --> 00:19:55.050
So video encoding-- so
once I have this video

00:19:55.050 --> 00:19:57.570
and I need to get it into
all the different formats,

00:19:57.570 --> 00:20:00.090
transcode it, we take
care of all of that.

00:20:00.090 --> 00:20:02.940
Whether you're doing
the set top delivery,

00:20:02.940 --> 00:20:06.000
or going to a Chromecast,
or going to your phone,

00:20:06.000 --> 00:20:07.140
we can take care of that.

00:20:07.140 --> 00:20:10.290
We can also work
with video overlays,

00:20:10.290 --> 00:20:14.430
embedding the captions,
putting in at the bottom

00:20:14.430 --> 00:20:16.590
there what are called
[INAUDIBLE] markers.

00:20:16.590 --> 00:20:19.170
Those are the markers, as the
video content's going along,

00:20:19.170 --> 00:20:21.150
it'll trigger ads to be played.

00:20:21.150 --> 00:20:26.600
So when it's creating the
video, so that it plays it out,

00:20:26.600 --> 00:20:28.520
we take into account
kind of everything

00:20:28.520 --> 00:20:33.400
you need to do with that video
and make sure it's ready to go,

00:20:33.400 --> 00:20:35.750
so that you can send it
out through your content

00:20:35.750 --> 00:20:36.820
distribution network.

00:20:39.810 --> 00:20:43.140
So syndication, I already
mentioned it just a second ago.

00:20:43.140 --> 00:20:46.030
But this is taking the
content now that you have

00:20:46.030 --> 00:20:49.110
and sending it out to all
the online players that

00:20:49.110 --> 00:20:51.600
need to receive your
content, so that then,

00:20:51.600 --> 00:20:54.450
somebody that's
subscribing maybe to a Hulu

00:20:54.450 --> 00:20:58.620
or even Netflix, you need
your movies up there.

00:20:58.620 --> 00:21:00.270
So you send it up to them.

00:21:00.270 --> 00:21:04.020
And then they'll be
able to play it there.

00:21:04.020 --> 00:21:06.900
So we syndicate over 1.5
million minutes a month

00:21:06.900 --> 00:21:10.110
on behalf of the customers
that use our content.

00:21:10.110 --> 00:21:12.270
And that's going to
all the leading portals

00:21:12.270 --> 00:21:14.880
around the world.

00:21:14.880 --> 00:21:16.490
So we have an OTT store.

00:21:16.490 --> 00:21:19.520
This is not just we're
going to build a store where

00:21:19.520 --> 00:21:21.380
you can do quick subscriptions.

00:21:21.380 --> 00:21:23.510
It's more tying
into applications

00:21:23.510 --> 00:21:26.990
where you need to, like,
authenticate against the cable

00:21:26.990 --> 00:21:28.550
subscription.

00:21:28.550 --> 00:21:30.290
So if you're a
sports app, and you

00:21:30.290 --> 00:21:33.440
need to make sure that
somebody is paying for a cable

00:21:33.440 --> 00:21:37.370
subscription like with
the TV Everywhere,

00:21:37.370 --> 00:21:40.220
this will help you tie
your application together

00:21:40.220 --> 00:21:43.350
with a subscription service.

00:21:43.350 --> 00:21:47.610
And then dynamic ad insertion,
so ad insertion at the server

00:21:47.610 --> 00:21:49.920
side is what Anvato does.

00:21:49.920 --> 00:21:52.530
There's obviously
client-side ad insertion.

00:21:52.530 --> 00:21:55.620
But to plug it into
the server side,

00:21:55.620 --> 00:21:59.640
you can do it right
within the Anvato stack.

00:21:59.640 --> 00:22:05.010
So again, you can put ads on
Apple TV, Chromecast, Roku,

00:22:05.010 --> 00:22:05.790
any VAST--

00:22:05.790 --> 00:22:08.310
V-A-S-T-- compliant
third-party ad servers,

00:22:08.310 --> 00:22:09.930
so the DoubleClick server.

00:22:09.930 --> 00:22:12.750
There's other servers out
there, like FreeWheel.

00:22:12.750 --> 00:22:16.260
We can pull from many
of those ad servers.

00:22:16.260 --> 00:22:18.180
And then generating
the analytics

00:22:18.180 --> 00:22:22.200
is obviously key, getting
the feedback for what ads

00:22:22.200 --> 00:22:26.540
are playing, and the completion,
and the click-through rates.

00:22:26.540 --> 00:22:28.370
So the one thing
with server-side ads

00:22:28.370 --> 00:22:32.120
I like to point out is
it's a little smoother user

00:22:32.120 --> 00:22:33.230
experience.

00:22:33.230 --> 00:22:35.780
Because it's not getting there,
having to go grab the ad,

00:22:35.780 --> 00:22:37.020
pull it back in.

00:22:37.020 --> 00:22:40.190
So when you're playing video,
the server-side ad insertion,

00:22:40.190 --> 00:22:43.040
it's already baked
into the video stream.

00:22:43.040 --> 00:22:44.340
And it'll just play right out.

00:22:47.130 --> 00:22:52.410
So then the player itself,
Anvato won't make a player.

00:22:52.410 --> 00:22:55.650
The actual skin, that's up
to you and your developers

00:22:55.650 --> 00:22:59.440
to make it look and brand
it however you want.

00:22:59.440 --> 00:23:04.350
But what Anvato players the
back-end SDKs and the APIs

00:23:04.350 --> 00:23:09.120
that you're going to use, will
allow you to have that player

00:23:09.120 --> 00:23:11.460
and be able to play
it on any device.

00:23:11.460 --> 00:23:13.710
It does really fast startup.

00:23:13.710 --> 00:23:16.380
And it'll insert the
ads we've talked about.

00:23:16.380 --> 00:23:18.870
It does both adapt
to bitrate streaming.

00:23:18.870 --> 00:23:23.740
And it can do Virtual
Reality, VR stream.

00:23:23.740 --> 00:23:27.390
So if you're going to
develop your player,

00:23:27.390 --> 00:23:29.010
we give you all
the back-end tools

00:23:29.010 --> 00:23:32.070
that you need to be able to pull
the content, play the content.

00:23:32.070 --> 00:23:34.770
You just need to develop the
application and use our APIs.

00:23:37.670 --> 00:23:39.590
And then there's a
reporting dashboard.

00:23:39.590 --> 00:23:41.750
There's a few changes going
on with the reporting.

00:23:41.750 --> 00:23:43.970
Now that we're part of
Google, Google obviously

00:23:43.970 --> 00:23:47.040
is very concerned about what
data is being collected.

00:23:47.040 --> 00:23:50.510
So Anvato is just lining up
with the Google Analytics Team

00:23:50.510 --> 00:23:54.860
and making sure the feedback
that we get within the Anvato

00:23:54.860 --> 00:23:58.460
platform matches up with the
feedback that Google collects,

00:23:58.460 --> 00:24:02.540
so a great reporting
engine as part of it.

00:24:02.540 --> 00:24:05.690
So a couple examples--
so that's the stack.

00:24:05.690 --> 00:24:07.100
That's the wheel.

00:24:07.100 --> 00:24:09.120
But what are a few things
you can do with it?

00:24:09.120 --> 00:24:13.160
So by being able to migrate
off of a satellite truck,

00:24:13.160 --> 00:24:16.430
going up over satellite,
hitting the data center,

00:24:16.430 --> 00:24:19.850
going to the service
bureaus, and then back out,

00:24:19.850 --> 00:24:22.250
you can now go from the Cloud.

00:24:22.250 --> 00:24:25.880
You have your encoder either
on-site or in the Cloud.

00:24:25.880 --> 00:24:27.830
You send the livestream there.

00:24:27.830 --> 00:24:30.230
You stream it out
to all the devices.

00:24:30.230 --> 00:24:33.650
You can then syndicate
it from the Cloud.

00:24:33.650 --> 00:24:37.520
So all of the sudden, your costs
of the truck and the satellite,

00:24:37.520 --> 00:24:39.060
you don't need that anymore.

00:24:39.060 --> 00:24:42.920
The real key, though,
that I've found

00:24:42.920 --> 00:24:48.410
is making sure you have enough
bandwidth between the venue

00:24:48.410 --> 00:24:50.510
and getting to the Cloud.

00:24:50.510 --> 00:24:55.130
And over the last two
years, almost every venue

00:24:55.130 --> 00:24:57.620
has been putting in more
and more bandwidth, which

00:24:57.620 --> 00:25:01.070
is going to allow this
to occur kind of anywhere

00:25:01.070 --> 00:25:01.970
is what I'm seeing.

00:25:01.970 --> 00:25:07.100
So big cost savings if you're
broadcasting content looking

00:25:07.100 --> 00:25:10.070
to move it over into the Cloud.

00:25:10.070 --> 00:25:13.670
And then a live
linear channel, so

00:25:13.670 --> 00:25:18.320
if you're broadcasting all day,
you need to have a schedule.

00:25:18.320 --> 00:25:19.400
And we create a schedule.

00:25:22.700 --> 00:25:26.300
Because with digital
rights, you're not always

00:25:26.300 --> 00:25:31.680
going to be able to broadcast on
your phone or out over the top

00:25:31.680 --> 00:25:35.180
the same content that you're
sending across onto the TV.

00:25:35.180 --> 00:25:38.240
So you can quickly
replace content

00:25:38.240 --> 00:25:40.130
that you don't have
the rights that you

00:25:40.130 --> 00:25:43.464
want to have the rights to
or with a different show

00:25:43.464 --> 00:25:44.630
that you have the rights to.

00:25:44.630 --> 00:25:49.940
So it's the quick, easy,
adaptable, live, linear

00:25:49.940 --> 00:25:55.970
streaming programming that
is also part of Anvato.

00:25:55.970 --> 00:25:59.480
And then this is where it's
kind of exciting what's

00:25:59.480 --> 00:26:01.100
coming around machine learning.

00:26:01.100 --> 00:26:04.970
Because now, if I
can personalize this

00:26:04.970 --> 00:26:08.480
with a pool of
content, then everybody

00:26:08.480 --> 00:26:11.040
could start to get
their own shows.

00:26:11.040 --> 00:26:13.580
And it's not just one show
going out to everybody

00:26:13.580 --> 00:26:15.660
using the same application.

00:26:15.660 --> 00:26:17.510
So this is one of
the areas that I

00:26:17.510 --> 00:26:20.967
think is going to benefit
greatly from machine learning,

00:26:20.967 --> 00:26:23.300
and being able to take all
the feedback from people that

00:26:23.300 --> 00:26:26.180
are watching your channels,
and be able to provide

00:26:26.180 --> 00:26:28.610
different content to them.

00:26:28.610 --> 00:26:32.840
So shifting gears from
Anvato, virtual reality

00:26:32.840 --> 00:26:37.040
is an area that Google's
invested in heavily.

00:26:37.040 --> 00:26:40.790
For anybody that was at
NAB, which is the big trade

00:26:40.790 --> 00:26:45.650
show in Las Vegas back in April,
Google will have a booth at IBC

00:26:45.650 --> 00:26:46.640
over in Amsterdam.

00:26:46.640 --> 00:26:48.980
I'm guessing a lot of
people here go to IBC.

00:26:48.980 --> 00:26:50.810
So we'll be back at IBC.

00:26:50.810 --> 00:26:52.550
But this camera was at NAB.

00:26:52.550 --> 00:26:53.270
It's really cool.

00:26:53.270 --> 00:26:54.680
It's about that big.

00:26:54.680 --> 00:26:56.210
You take the picture.

00:26:56.210 --> 00:26:58.580
And then we don't
build the camera.

00:26:58.580 --> 00:27:01.410
But what Google does is
they built the software--

00:27:01.410 --> 00:27:02.810
it's called Jump--

00:27:02.810 --> 00:27:05.090
that allows you to upload--

00:27:05.090 --> 00:27:08.300
I think there's 34 cameras--

00:27:08.300 --> 00:27:09.770
and upload all the content.

00:27:09.770 --> 00:27:11.880
And then it does the stitching.

00:27:11.880 --> 00:27:14.180
So if you're in VR,
you can do that.

00:27:14.180 --> 00:27:17.150
And then anybody that saw
the release the other day,

00:27:17.150 --> 00:27:21.890
Google announced VR, like,
180 instead of VR 360.

00:27:21.890 --> 00:27:26.030
Because 360 allows you to
look all the way around.

00:27:26.030 --> 00:27:28.880
But if you look
back there and you

00:27:28.880 --> 00:27:31.730
have a lot of cameras shooting
the content, all of the sudden,

00:27:31.730 --> 00:27:34.790
you see everybody making the
movie or making the content.

00:27:34.790 --> 00:27:37.880
With 180, you can
look around this way.

00:27:37.880 --> 00:27:42.230
But you're only seeing what the
movie makers want you to see.

00:27:42.230 --> 00:27:44.930
So I think it's--

00:27:44.930 --> 00:27:46.980
I haven't-- this is
just from what I read.

00:27:46.980 --> 00:27:49.530
But I think it's a way of
trying to say, you know what?

00:27:49.530 --> 00:27:52.910
Let's adopt a technology
that is really cool,

00:27:52.910 --> 00:27:55.470
in terms of being able to
pan around in the video,

00:27:55.470 --> 00:27:59.400
but not allow you to go back
and see how it was being made.

00:27:59.400 --> 00:28:02.780
So VR 180 just was
announced a couple days ago.

00:28:02.780 --> 00:28:05.480
And I'm sure cameras
like this will figure out

00:28:05.480 --> 00:28:08.840
ways to upload it to
Jump and then just render

00:28:08.840 --> 00:28:11.790
the front half of it.

00:28:11.790 --> 00:28:14.380
Daydream-- so the
goggles, so that you

00:28:14.380 --> 00:28:16.590
can look at the VR experience.

00:28:16.590 --> 00:28:20.560
These are the goggles
that Google creates.

00:28:20.560 --> 00:28:23.650
And then kind off of
products back to the core

00:28:23.650 --> 00:28:28.120
Cloud that I think is probably
the most interesting part.

00:28:28.120 --> 00:28:32.800
If you kind of have built
already everything that

00:28:32.800 --> 00:28:37.650
I've showed you today, BigQuery,
the cloud-based analytics--

00:28:37.650 --> 00:28:42.080
so as we start to
run machine learning

00:28:42.080 --> 00:28:47.180
against the archives
of all the video

00:28:47.180 --> 00:28:50.510
that we own, and we start to
pull out metadata, and more

00:28:50.510 --> 00:28:55.580
metadata, and more metadata, if
you can get it in to BigQuery

00:28:55.580 --> 00:28:58.310
and then start to be able
to run analytics on it,

00:28:58.310 --> 00:29:00.290
I think that's going to
be where the power is,

00:29:00.290 --> 00:29:02.870
combining the machine
learning with the analytics,

00:29:02.870 --> 00:29:07.100
and being able to
pull out queries of,

00:29:07.100 --> 00:29:11.060
I need video with this actor
or this actress in here

00:29:11.060 --> 00:29:13.340
over the last five years,
and be able to quickly

00:29:13.340 --> 00:29:19.080
pull all that content out and
create new content with it.

00:29:19.080 --> 00:29:26.520
So BigQuery is our enterprise
warehouse for this large data.

00:29:26.520 --> 00:29:27.720
Spotify uses it.

00:29:27.720 --> 00:29:29.430
I think you've
probably seen this

00:29:29.430 --> 00:29:31.249
in some other presentations.

00:29:31.249 --> 00:29:32.790
But the reason I
like to point it out

00:29:32.790 --> 00:29:35.700
from a media and
entertainment perspective

00:29:35.700 --> 00:29:40.230
is sometimes you're
asking simple questions.

00:29:40.230 --> 00:29:42.000
One of the questions
I read about online

00:29:42.000 --> 00:29:46.250
in terms of Spotify was, what
are just the top 10 songs that

00:29:46.250 --> 00:29:48.650
played in Spain for October?

00:29:48.650 --> 00:29:50.880
And it was taking
them 44 minutes

00:29:50.880 --> 00:29:52.890
to get the answer back on that.

00:29:52.890 --> 00:29:55.200
When they moved
over into BigQuery,

00:29:55.200 --> 00:29:57.690
that frame went
down to a minute.

00:29:57.690 --> 00:29:59.580
So then you think about
the business analyst

00:29:59.580 --> 00:30:03.270
trying to decide, what are we
going to advertise in Spain?

00:30:03.270 --> 00:30:05.820
What are we going to
do for the next month?

00:30:05.820 --> 00:30:09.060
If they can ask more
questions of the data

00:30:09.060 --> 00:30:11.070
and get the responses
back quicker,

00:30:11.070 --> 00:30:14.170
obviously, it's
really going to help.

00:30:14.170 --> 00:30:17.520
So BigQuery is that
platform layer that I think

00:30:17.520 --> 00:30:20.190
is going to help media and
entertainment customers.

00:30:20.190 --> 00:30:24.390
But it's part of the
basic Cloud, not an add-on

00:30:24.390 --> 00:30:28.080
like Anvato, that was acquired.

00:30:28.080 --> 00:30:29.870
So what's the next wave?

00:30:29.870 --> 00:30:32.690
We've all been talking
about machine learning.

00:30:32.690 --> 00:30:34.970
And so everybody,
I think, has seen

00:30:34.970 --> 00:30:40.370
that there's the five APIs
that are available today.

00:30:40.370 --> 00:30:46.040
So the speech ones, working
on grabbing that text out

00:30:46.040 --> 00:30:49.490
for closed captioning, and then
taking that closed captioning,

00:30:49.490 --> 00:30:52.160
turning that into metadata
that you can search,

00:30:52.160 --> 00:30:54.440
and then also
translating it into all

00:30:54.440 --> 00:30:58.370
the different languages
is, obviously, a big focus.

00:30:58.370 --> 00:31:03.230
And then the Cloud
Video Intelligence API

00:31:03.230 --> 00:31:05.430
is going to be used quite a bit.

00:31:05.430 --> 00:31:09.500
So what we did is we took
the video supply chain here

00:31:09.500 --> 00:31:11.750
and came up with a
few of the concepts

00:31:11.750 --> 00:31:16.370
that we're working on
to plug into Anvato.

00:31:16.370 --> 00:31:21.160
So today, the machine learning
is not built into Anvato yet.

00:31:21.160 --> 00:31:23.740
But this is what we're
working on for future versions

00:31:23.740 --> 00:31:25.960
of the OTT.

00:31:25.960 --> 00:31:29.160
So a couple that I
like to highlight,

00:31:29.160 --> 00:31:30.460
like in Cloud Editing--

00:31:33.310 --> 00:31:36.350
I hope machine learning
doesn't replace all the jobs,

00:31:36.350 --> 00:31:42.200
and that it allows us to
display more content--

00:31:42.200 --> 00:31:46.370
kind of back to my first
comments, which is,

00:31:46.370 --> 00:31:48.350
there's more content
available, because there's

00:31:48.350 --> 00:31:50.060
more ways to consume it.

00:31:50.060 --> 00:31:53.250
And I can see
exactly what I want.

00:31:53.250 --> 00:31:58.760
So I was down at NASCAR, one
of the race series in the US.

00:31:58.760 --> 00:32:02.390
But basically, think about
Formula One over here.

00:32:02.390 --> 00:32:06.290
If your favorite
driver or favorite car

00:32:06.290 --> 00:32:10.010
doesn't come in the top
three or so of that day,

00:32:10.010 --> 00:32:11.930
more than likely, if
you log on that night

00:32:11.930 --> 00:32:15.380
and you don't watch the race,
there's almost no content

00:32:15.380 --> 00:32:17.450
available to watch.

00:32:17.450 --> 00:32:22.220
Because somebody took all the
video that was shot that day

00:32:22.220 --> 00:32:26.690
and made a couple really
cool highlights of the race.

00:32:26.690 --> 00:32:28.740
But that means, my
favorite driver,

00:32:28.740 --> 00:32:31.190
I can't see any
content about him.

00:32:31.190 --> 00:32:35.550
But every one of those cars
has video cameras on it,

00:32:35.550 --> 00:32:39.380
one probably on the driver now,
usually one facing forward,

00:32:39.380 --> 00:32:41.600
a camera facing backwards.

00:32:41.600 --> 00:32:44.990
So it's not that we didn't
capture the content.

00:32:44.990 --> 00:32:49.070
It's that we didn't have
enough editors to go around

00:32:49.070 --> 00:32:51.610
to edit every single one.

00:32:51.610 --> 00:32:55.760
Well, imagine if you could
bring all that content in right

00:32:55.760 --> 00:32:59.840
at the end of a race, run it
through a machine learning kind

00:32:59.840 --> 00:33:05.570
of a cloud editing intelligent
clipping, so it was very quick.

00:33:05.570 --> 00:33:08.060
And for every
single race car, you

00:33:08.060 --> 00:33:12.924
could produce a five-minute
clip of the races.

00:33:12.924 --> 00:33:14.312
Well, that's a lot.

00:33:14.312 --> 00:33:15.770
Because, as editing,
you would have

00:33:15.770 --> 00:33:17.480
to spend a lot of time looking.

00:33:17.480 --> 00:33:20.090
But if you can train it
on what's interesting,

00:33:20.090 --> 00:33:24.890
when it made passes, when it
went into the pit stops, all

00:33:24.890 --> 00:33:28.040
the things that you might
want to see about the race,

00:33:28.040 --> 00:33:31.520
imagine you could then have--

00:33:31.520 --> 00:33:33.740
let's say there's
40 cars in a race--

00:33:33.740 --> 00:33:35.480
40 different clips.

00:33:35.480 --> 00:33:38.600
Maybe you could charge
people a small subscription

00:33:38.600 --> 00:33:41.840
to go in and be able to view
all the clips at the end

00:33:41.840 --> 00:33:43.220
of my favorite driver.

00:33:43.220 --> 00:33:47.150
So I could click on it, watch
the video, pay a little bit.

00:33:47.150 --> 00:33:48.560
Or it's ad-supported.

00:33:48.560 --> 00:33:49.850
Maybe there's ads.

00:33:49.850 --> 00:33:53.060
And now instead of everybody
just watching one highlight,

00:33:53.060 --> 00:33:55.100
everybody can go
and pick and choose

00:33:55.100 --> 00:33:57.200
which highlight
they want to watch,

00:33:57.200 --> 00:33:59.774
because that's the driver
they're interested in.

00:33:59.774 --> 00:34:01.190
And more than
likely, they're also

00:34:01.190 --> 00:34:04.430
going to still go watch the
highlights of the overall race.

00:34:04.430 --> 00:34:06.770
Because of course, we
want to see who can race.

00:34:06.770 --> 00:34:10.940
So these are the things, in my
mind, that I get excited about.

00:34:10.940 --> 00:34:14.030
Because all of the
sudden, there's

00:34:14.030 --> 00:34:16.699
more content available,
easier to get to it.

00:34:16.699 --> 00:34:17.600
It's edited.

00:34:17.600 --> 00:34:21.880
It's gone through and
created better highlights.

00:34:21.880 --> 00:34:25.449
So that's, in my mind,
a kind of forward

00:34:25.449 --> 00:34:30.280
customer-facing implementation
of machine learning.

00:34:30.280 --> 00:34:34.690
Under the encoding, if we can
do a better job with encoding

00:34:34.690 --> 00:34:36.310
as the machine learns--

00:34:36.310 --> 00:34:38.590
so if I'm an announcer
and I'm just standing here

00:34:38.590 --> 00:34:41.380
like I am here,
encoding, you should

00:34:41.380 --> 00:34:43.719
be able to squeeze this
video down pretty far.

00:34:43.719 --> 00:34:45.590
Because there's not much action.

00:34:45.590 --> 00:34:48.670
But as soon as action starts,
slowed down the encoding,

00:34:48.670 --> 00:34:50.830
so you get a
better-quality signal.

00:34:50.830 --> 00:34:53.739
So I think on sporting
events or events that

00:34:53.739 --> 00:34:57.050
switch from almost no
action to a lot of action,

00:34:57.050 --> 00:34:59.320
if you can have
machines watching that

00:34:59.320 --> 00:35:03.370
and being able to encode
up and down, on the fly,

00:35:03.370 --> 00:35:06.120
we're going to get
better compression.

00:35:06.120 --> 00:35:08.820
And then there's a
whole host of other ways

00:35:08.820 --> 00:35:11.830
we're looking at plugging
in the machine learning.

00:35:11.830 --> 00:35:14.580
So again, the Cloud Vision,
you might have seen this.

00:35:14.580 --> 00:35:17.820
So on stills, face
detection, pulling that out,

00:35:17.820 --> 00:35:23.630
the logo, identification
labels, landmark OCR.

00:35:23.630 --> 00:35:26.415
And then this was on
my highlights example.

00:35:29.300 --> 00:35:35.180
"Chopped" is a chef TV
show over in the US--

00:35:35.180 --> 00:35:38.440
but being able to
take machine learning,

00:35:38.440 --> 00:35:43.580
beat it full of clips over the
hour, put them in, and say,

00:35:43.580 --> 00:35:47.530
give me a five minute
highlight for "Chopped."

00:35:47.530 --> 00:35:49.220
There's some shows.

00:35:49.220 --> 00:35:53.580
Discovery, they
shoot up in Alaska.

00:35:53.580 --> 00:35:55.660
The crab fishermen
are out there.

00:35:55.660 --> 00:35:58.540
I think I've heard, they shoot
like 500 hours or 1,000 hours

00:35:58.540 --> 00:36:00.370
of content for every show.

00:36:00.370 --> 00:36:02.320
Because they have so
many boats going out.

00:36:02.320 --> 00:36:04.361
And being able to run it
through machine learning

00:36:04.361 --> 00:36:07.460
and be able to help create
more personalized highlights

00:36:07.460 --> 00:36:10.536
is going to definitely be a
use case for machine learning.

00:36:10.536 --> 00:36:12.160
And then the one that
everybody's still

00:36:12.160 --> 00:36:14.800
trying to figure out and
how to do a better job

00:36:14.800 --> 00:36:17.620
is contextual ads
and micro-segment,

00:36:17.620 --> 00:36:19.950
tagging those videos
using machine learning.

00:36:19.950 --> 00:36:23.140
So whatever's
playing in the video,

00:36:23.140 --> 00:36:29.110
can you pull out related content
out of the video, specifically?

00:36:29.110 --> 00:36:31.034
And with these
Vision APIs, we're

00:36:31.034 --> 00:36:32.200
going to be able to do that.

00:36:32.200 --> 00:36:37.280
So the last one, the
closed captioning

00:36:37.280 --> 00:36:39.700
I talked about a
little bit-- so being

00:36:39.700 --> 00:36:42.280
able to take the
speech-to-text and then

00:36:42.280 --> 00:36:43.990
be able to turn
that into metadata,

00:36:43.990 --> 00:36:48.250
so that you can search the
text and be able to find

00:36:48.250 --> 00:36:50.090
the content that you want.

00:36:50.090 --> 00:36:53.770
So machine learning, it's
definitely in its infancy

00:36:53.770 --> 00:36:56.050
right now in this industry.

00:36:56.050 --> 00:36:58.230
Those APIs are available today.

00:36:58.230 --> 00:37:00.280
There's some good
tutorials on it

00:37:00.280 --> 00:37:02.440
if you want to start
to learn about them.

00:37:02.440 --> 00:37:07.390
And then if you're interested in
the Anvato software or the ZYNC

00:37:07.390 --> 00:37:10.770
software, definitely contact
your local Google rep.

00:37:10.770 --> 00:37:12.130
They can get in touch with us.

00:37:12.130 --> 00:37:15.020
We can do meetings with
you and help you out there.

00:37:15.020 --> 00:37:19.190
So with that, I've got a
few minutes for questions.

00:37:19.190 --> 00:37:20.570
Thanks so much for your time.

00:37:20.570 --> 00:37:24.670
Any questions?

00:37:24.670 --> 00:37:26.120
Does anybody speak English?

00:37:26.120 --> 00:37:26.720
I hope you do.

00:37:29.460 --> 00:37:32.790
So I'll be back here if
there aren't any questions.

00:37:32.790 --> 00:37:34.470
But thanks so much
for your time.

00:37:34.470 --> 00:37:37.230
And enjoy the rest
of Google Next.

00:37:37.230 --> 00:37:39.080
[APPLAUSE]

